21. FIRESTORE DATA STRUCTURE
Design an appropriate Firestore structure.
At minimum, consider:
staff
attendance
attendanceSessions
branches
departments
roles
performanceRatings
auditLogs
settings
Use normalized references where appropriate.
Example staff document:
{
staffCode: "KSP-137-052-1025",
firstName: "Jean",
lastName: "Claude",
gender: "male",
nationality: "rwandan",
department: "trainer",
educationLevel: "masters",
certificateRange: "6-10",
branch: "kigali",
employeeNumber: 25,
jobTitle: "Trainer",
employmentStatus: "active",
createdAt: timestamp,
updatedAt: timestamp
}
Attendance records must reference the permanent internal staff ID.
Do not depend solely on Staff Code.
________________________________________
22. SECURITY
Implement Firebase Authentication and Firestore Security Rules.
Users must only be able to access information according to their role.
At minimum support:
•	Owner
•	Manager
•	Accountant/authorized staff
•	Attendance operator
•	Staff member
Owner:
Full access.

Manager:
Staff management and attendance/reporting according to assigned permissions.

Attendance Operator:
Can scan and record attendance but should not automatically have access to confidential staff information.

Staff:
Can view their permitted personal information and attendance information.
Do not expose sensitive employee information through unrestricted Firestore queries.
Validate permissions both in the UI and Firebase Security Rules.
Never rely only on frontend hiding.
________________________________________
23. ATTENDANCE CORRECTIONS
Create an administrative attendance correction mechanism.
Authorized users should be able to correct:
•	Missing IN
•	Missing OUT
•	Incorrect scan
•	Wrong date
•	Wrong session
Every correction must create an audit record containing:
•	Who made the correction
•	What was changed
•	Previous value
•	New value
•	Date/time
•	Reason
Never silently modify historical attendance.
